# Design Core -- AI Guidelines

## What This Repo Is

Design Core is a personal, file-based design tool. One repo, one owner (Brandon), many companies. Brandon both maintains the tool and designs in it, so two kinds of work happen here and both are allowed:

- **Tool changes** -- root HTML pages (`index.html`, `project.html`, `canvas.html`, `prototype.html`, `design-system.html`, `captures.html`), `public/scripts/` (browser JS), `public/styles/` (`shared.css`, `ds.css`, `app.css`), repo-root `scripts/` (Node CLIs and the dev-server API), `vite.config.js`, `.cursor/rules/`, `docs/`.
- **Design work** -- everything under `public/data/companies/<company-slug>/`: projects, screens, prototypes, design system components, captures.

Say which one you are doing when it is not obvious. Speak technically and directly. There is no backend, no database, no framework: the tool is vanilla HTML/CSS/JS served by Vite at http://localhost:3000.

## Companies

All design data lives at `public/data/companies/<company-slug>/` with these subfolders:

- `projects/` -- `index.json` plus one folder per project (see "Project Structure")
- `design-system/` -- `company.css`, `registry.json`, `components/<company-slug>/`, plus any asset folders
- `captures/` -- `config.json`, `manifest.json`, screenshots
- `users/` -- per-user preferences (managed by the tool, do not hand-edit)

`public/data/companies/index.json` lists the companies: `{ "companies": [{ "slug", "name", "createdAt" }] }`. `public/data/site.json` (optional `publicBaseUrl` for share links) stays at the top level.

**Active company in the UI:** the home page has a company picker; the left rail has a company switcher at the top (with "add new company"); every tool page accepts `?company=<slug>` and remembers the last choice in the browser. Share links include `&company=`.

**Choosing the company as the AI:** if the user names a company, use it. Otherwise read `public/data/companies/index.json`; if there is exactly one, use it; if there are several and context does not make it clear, ask. Project ids only need to be unique within a company.

**Dev server API** (all company-scoped via `?company=<slug>`): `/api/project-admin`, `/api/projects-summary`, `/api/user-prefs`, `/api/duplicate-screen`, `/api/capture/launch`. Plus `/api/companies` (GET) and `/api/company-admin` (POST `create-company`).

**npm scripts:** `dev`, `build`, `preview`, `capture` (`npm run capture -- --company <slug>`, auto-picks when there is one company), `sync-public-url`, `doctor` (checks Node, deps, and every company's JSON, canvas entries, and CSS link depth), `import-company` (`npm run import-company -- <path-to-old-repo> <slug> "<Name>"` copies an old single-company repo's `public/data` into a company folder and fixes relative links).

`.designer` (gitignored) may exist with `name` and `team`; it only feeds the "created by" attribution picker. Do not build any flow around it.

### Git rules

- **NEVER commit or push without the user explicitly asking.** Do not auto-commit, do not commit as part of a multi-step task, do not commit "to save progress."
- **NEVER run `git fetch`, `git pull`, `git merge`, or any command that syncs with a remote** without the user explicitly asking.

### Copy rules

- No em-dashes in copy or UI text. Use commas, periods, or colons instead.
- Code comments are single-line.

## What Designers Build Here

- **Canvas screens** -- Static HTML/CSS files for UI ideation. No JavaScript.
- **Prototypes** -- Interactive HTML/CSS/JS mini apps that feel like real features. JavaScript is expected.

## How paths work

Vite serves the `public/` folder as the web root. All data files live under `public/` on disk, but are accessed without the `public/` prefix in the browser and in fetch URLs.

- **Disk path**: `public/data/companies/acme/projects/my-app/project.json`
- **Browser/fetch path**: `data/companies/acme/projects/my-app/project.json`

When creating or editing files, always use the **full disk path** (with `public/`). When referencing URLs in HTML `src`, `href`, or `fetch()` calls, omit `public/`.

### Stylesheet link depth (get this right)

Relative CSS links must climb back to the site root. The depth depends on where the file lives:

| File | Tool CSS (`shared.css`, `ds.css`) | Company CSS |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Canvas screen `.../projects/<p>/screens/x.html` | six `../` (`../../../../../../styles/shared.css`) | `../../../design-system/company.css` |
| Prototype `.../projects/<p>/prototypes/<x>/index.html` | seven `../` (`../../../../../../../styles/shared.css`) | `../../../../design-system/company.css` |
| Design system component fragment | none (loaded into `design-system.html` at the site root) | none; reference assets page-relative, e.g. `data/companies/<slug>/design-system/...` |

`npm run doctor` flags wrong depths.

## Project Structure

All paths below are **disk paths** (relative to repo root). `<c>` is the company slug.

- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/` -- One folder per project. The **project id** is the folder name (e.g. `my-app`).
- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/project.json` -- **Project name and metadata.** Contains `name`, `description`, and optionally `createdBy`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` (shown on the project hub with relative dates when present). Set `updatedAt` when the project meaningfully changes: the **home** page uses it for "Recently updated" sorting.
- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/index.json` -- List of all projects in the company (`id`, `name`, `description`, `createdBy`, `createdAt`). The home page also loads each project's `project.json`, `canvas.json`, and `prototypes/index.json` for search, sort, and screen/prototype counts on cards.
- `public/data/site.json` -- Optional tool config: `{ "publicBaseUrl": "https://org.github.io/repo/" }` so **Copy link** for prototypes uses the GitHub Pages URL while developing on localhost. Run **`npm run sync-public-url`** to fill it from `git remote get-url origin`. On `*.github.io`, the base is inferred from the current URL if this is omitted.
- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/canvas.json` -- **Canvas screen layout.** Lists which screens appear on the canvas and their positions/sizes. Contains `{ "screens": [{ "file": "home.html", "x": 0, "y": 0, "width": 390 }, ...] }`. When adding a new screen, you MUST also add an entry here or it won't show on the canvas. Standard widths: **390** for mobile, **1024** for tablet, **1280** for desktop. Use whatever fits the screen being designed.
- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/screens/` -- Static screen HTML files for the ideation canvas
- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/prototypes/<prototype>/index.html` -- Interactive prototype
- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/prototypes/<prototype>/meta.json` -- Prototype name and description; optional `thumbnail` (filename or path under the prototype folder, e.g. `preview.png`) for the project page card image. Optional `externalTestUrl` (string): full **http://** or **https://** URL to Maze, UserTesting, etc. The tool shows **Tested** only when this parses as a plausible web URL (hostname with a dot, or `localhost`).
- `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/prototypes/index.json` -- Prototype list (`id`, `name`, `description`, optional `device`). Device can be `"mobile"` (phone frame, 390x844), `"online"` (small desktop frame, 1280x800), `"desktop"` (1024x768), or `"responsive"` (fluid).
- `public/data/companies/<c>/design-system/components/<c>/` -- Design system component HTML previews for the company. Always use the company subfolder.
- `public/data/companies/<c>/design-system/registry.json` -- Category list + groups (order controls layout).
- `public/data/companies/<c>/design-system/company.css` -- Brand overrides for colors, fonts, radii, plus company component classes. Auto-loaded after `shared.css` and `ds.css`.
- `public/styles/shared.css` -- Design tokens (colors, fonts, spacing, radii). Tool file, shared by every company.
- `public/styles/ds.css` -- Reusable component styles. Tool file, shared by every company.

Put brand-specific styling in `company.css`, not in `shared.css` or `ds.css`, so one company's brand does not leak into another. Editing the tool CSS is fine when the change is meant for every company.

### Design System Page Layout

The design system page (`design-system.html`) uses an **infinite canvas** with a registry-driven masonry layout. Categories pack into balanced vertical columns so short sections do not inherit empty space from taller neighbors.

- **`groups`** -- top-level libraries or themes. Groups normally stack vertically; groups with the same `pair` render side by side.
- **Light/dark themes** -- set `theme` to `"dark"` or `"light"` and give both groups the same `pair`. Each group becomes one continuous flat themed surface; categories use internal headings rather than card shells. IDs/names containing "dark" and "light" are inferred for backward compatibility.
- **Group layout** -- masonry is the default. Configure top-level `layout` defaults or a group's `layout` with `columns`, `columnWidth`, `maxColumnWidth`, and `gap`.
- **Categories** -- each has `group`, `name`, and `components`. Add `section` to the first category of a major chapter to create a full-width divider; use `span: "all"` for a wide visual anchor and optional `column` hints to keep related categories together.
- **Component layout** -- `"layout": "row"` still means the components inside one category sit side by side. A multi-component row automatically becomes full width unless explicitly constrained.
- **Full reference** -- read `docs/design-system.md` before creating or restructuring a company design system.

## Critical Rules

1. **Work in the right company.** Confirm the company slug before creating anything (see "Companies"). Never write one company's design work into another company's folder.
2. **Use the design system.** Always use existing CSS classes from `public/styles/ds.css`, design tokens from `public/styles/shared.css`, and the company's `company.css` and components. Read them before creating anything.
3. **Only the Phosphor `regular` and `fill` icon weights are loaded** (`company.css` imports just those two from `@phosphor-icons/web`). Any other weight renders as a blank box, NOT an error. A plain checkmark must be `ph ph-check` (regular): `ph-bold ph-check` and `ph-fill ph-check` both render empty; use `ph-fill ph-check-square` only when you want the boxed check. Icon weight comes from the class (`ph` / `ph-fill`), never from CSS `font-weight`.
4. **Mount canvas screens before building them.** When creating a new canvas screen, you MUST do these two things FIRST, before writing any real screen content, and in this order: (a) create the HTML file containing only an empty wrapper `<div style="background: var(--bg-0);"></div>`, then (b) add the entry to `canvas.json`. ONLY after both of those exist do you write the real screen content by editing the same file. This is non-negotiable: it makes the card appear on the canvas immediately so the screen fills in live. If you write the finished HTML first and then add it to `canvas.json` at the end, you have violated this rule. When creating multiple screens, mount ALL of their empty placeholders + `canvas.json` entries first, then fill them in one at a time. See "Creating a new canvas screen" below for the full procedure.
5. **Prototypes with multiple views use hash routing.** See "Prototype Mode".

## Before Creating Anything

When asked to create screens, prototypes, or any design work, **check what exists first**.

1. **Pick the company** (see "Companies"), then read `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/index.json` to see what projects already exist.
2. **If the user explicitly says "create a new project"**, proceed with the project creation flow below.
3. **If the user does NOT mention creating a new project** (e.g. "make me a home screen" or "design a login flow"), list the existing projects and ask where the work should go, unless it is obvious from context.
4. **If there are no projects yet**, say so and ask for a name.
5. **Never auto-generate a project name** like "New Project" or "Untitled". Always ask.

## How to Create Things

### Creating a new project

1. Choose a project id (lowercase, hyphenated, e.g. `mobile-redesign`), unique within the company
2. Create the folder: `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<id>/`
3. Create `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<id>/project.json`:
   ```json
   {
     "name": "Mobile Redesign",
     "description": "...",
     "createdBy": "Brandon Unglaub",
     "createdAt": "2026-03-15T00:00:00Z",
     "updatedAt": "2026-03-15T00:00:00Z"
   }
   ```
   (`updatedAt` optional; omit on first creation or mirror `createdAt`. Refresh it when the project changes so the home page sort stays accurate.)
4. Add an entry to `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/index.json` with matching `id`, `name`, `description`, `createdBy`, `createdAt`
5. Create `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<id>/canvas.json`: `{ "screens": [] }`
6. Create `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<id>/screens/` directory
7. Create `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<id>/prototypes/index.json`: `{ "prototypes": [] }`

### Creating a new canvas screen

**Mount the screen on the canvas first, then build it in place.** An empty card should appear immediately and the screen should fill in as you work, not wait for the whole file to be written before anything shows up. Edits to a file that already exists stream into the canvas live; new screens need the same feel, which means the file and the `canvas.json` entry must exist before you write the real content.

1. **Create a minimal placeholder HTML file** at `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/screens/<name>.html`. Just the wrapper, empty inside:
   ```html
   <div style="background: var(--bg-0);"></div>
   ```
   Write this first, as its own step, before doing anything else for this screen.
2. **Immediately add an entry to `canvas.json`** -- this is what makes the (currently empty) card appear on the canvas:
   ```json
   { "file": "<name>.html", "x": 0, "y": 0, "width": 390 }
   ```
   Space screens apart by adjusting `x` (add the width + 40px gap for each new screen). Standard widths: **390** for mobile, **1024** for tablet, **1280** for desktop. Match the width to what the screen is designed for: a desktop dashboard should be 1280, a mobile screen should be 390.
3. **Now build the screen by editing that same file.** Replace the empty wrapper with the real content. Each edit streams live into the card on the canvas, so prefer one larger write of the finished screen over many tiny edits (each edit causes the card to refresh). Link the stylesheets, then use the standard screen wrapper:
   ```html
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="../../../../../../styles/shared.css" />
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="../../../../../../styles/ds.css" />
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="../../../design-system/company.css" />
   <div style="background: var(--bg-0);">
     <!-- Screen content -->
   </div>
   ```
   **Path depth:** exactly **six** `../` for tool CSS and **three** for `company.css`. The wrapper is just a background container. Do NOT add `padding`, `max-width`, or `margin: 0 auto` to it: those prevent full-width elements (nav bars, hero backgrounds, footers) from reaching the edges. Individual content sections inside the wrapper handle their own padding (e.g. `padding: 16px`).
4. **When creating multiple new screens at once**, mount all of them as empty placeholders + `canvas.json` entries first (so every card shows up on the canvas right away), then fill them in one at a time. Don't build screen 1 to completion before screen 2's card even exists.
5. **HTML + CSS only.** No JavaScript. No interactivity.
#### Canvas screen content rules

Screens render inside an iframe at exactly the width set in `canvas.json`. Content that overflows will be clipped by the device frame. Follow these rules to avoid layout breakage:

- **Do NOT use `min-height: 100vh`.** The iframe auto-sizes to fit the content height. Using `100vh` creates unnecessarily tall cards on the canvas.
- **Do NOT use full HTML document structure** (`<!DOCTYPE>`, `<html>`, `<head>`, `<body>`). Canvas screens are loaded into iframes that handle this. Write the screen as a simple HTML fragment with a root wrapper `<div>`. If you need design system styles, add `<link>` tags before the wrapper -- but do NOT wrap in `<html>`/`<body>`.
- **Use flexible layouts for label/value rows.** Never use fixed-width grid columns (e.g. `grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr`) for label/value pairs -- long values like addresses or account names will clip. Instead use flexbox with `justify-content: space-between` and let the value side wrap naturally:
  ```html
  <div
    style="display:flex; justify-content:space-between; align-items:flex-start; gap:12px; padding:10px 0;"
  >
    <span style="...label styles...">Label</span>
    <span style="...value styles...; text-align:right;">Value text here</span>
  </div>
  ```
- **Use design system classes** over inline styles whenever a matching class exists (e.g. use `jh-row`, `jh-card`, `jh-label` if the company has defined them, or `ds-list-item` from the base system). Only use inline styles for layout composition or one-off spacing.
- **Do NOT hardcode `max-width` on value text** (e.g. `max-width: 55%`). Let the flex layout handle wrapping. If a value is long, the right-aligned text will wrap naturally within the available space.
- **When creating multiple screen variants** for ideation, vary the composition and visual hierarchy (card grouping, section structure, emphasis on different elements) -- do NOT reinvent the underlying row/label mechanics each time. Reuse the same label/value pattern across variants.
- **Do not show horizontal scrollbars** on canvas screens (or prototypes). Horizontal strips (chips, carousels, story rails) should still scroll sideways via touch/trackpad/shift+wheel, but the scrollbar gutter should be hidden. Add the utility class **`ds-scroll-x`** from `shared.css` to those scroll containers (it sets `overflow-x: auto` and hides scrollbars in Firefox, WebKit, and IE). Prefer **`overflow-x: clip`** (or `hidden`) on the screen root if needed so the document itself never gains a horizontal scrollbar. **Vertical** page scrolling is normal on desktop -- do not hide the main vertical scrollbar unless the design is an intentional full-screen app shell with its own scroller.

### Creating a new prototype

1. Ask: "Should this be mobile or online (desktop)?" Only ask if not already specified. "online", "desktop", or "web" means `"device": "online"`. "mobile", "phone", or "app" means `"device": "mobile"`. The `responsive` option also exists but is rarely needed.
2. Choose a prototype id (lowercase, hyphenated, e.g. `signup-flow`)
3. Create the folder: `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/prototypes/<id>/`
4. Create `meta.json`:
 ```json
 { "name": "Signup Flow", "description": "...", "thumbnail": "preview.png", "externalTestUrl": "https://t.maze.co/…" }
 ```
 (`thumbnail` is optional: image file in the prototype folder, used on the project hub. `externalTestUrl` is optional: must be a valid **http(s)** URL for the **Tested** badge and project counts.)
5. Add an entry to `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/prototypes/index.json`:
   ```json
   {
     "id": "<id>",
     "name": "Signup Flow",
     "description": "...",
     "device": "mobile"
   }
   ```
6. Create `index.html` with design system imports:
   ```html
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="../../../../../../../styles/shared.css" />
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="../../../../../../../styles/ds.css" />
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="../../../../design-system/company.css" />
   ```
   **Path depth:** use exactly **seven** `../` segments for the tool CSS and **four** for `company.css`. The prototype file lives at `data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/prototypes/<id>/index.html` under the site root; one level off and the styles silently fail (sometimes only on GitHub project Pages, where an extra level escapes the repo base).

## Prototype Mode -- Interactive Features

Files in `public/data/companies/<c>/projects/<project>/prototypes/<prototype>/` are interactive prototypes.

### How to detect prototype mode

The designer is in prototype mode when the file path matches `prototypes/*/`.

### What a prototype is

A prototype is a **self-contained interactive mini web app**. It lives in a single `index.html` (or a small set of files in the same folder). The designer prompts you to build it, and it handles its own:

- Form state (inputs, validation, error messages)
- Navigation between views/steps (tabs, multi-step flows)
- Animations and transitions
- Conditional rendering (show/hide based on state)
- Any interactive behavior that makes it feel like a real app feature

### Rules for prototype mode

1. **Read context first.** Read `meta.json` in the prototype folder to understand the prototype's name and purpose.
2. **JavaScript is expected.** Prototypes are interactive -- write real JS for state, transitions, validation, etc.
3. **Use hash routing for navigation.** Every prototype with multiple views/steps **must** use `location.hash` so each view gets a unique URL. This is required for usability testing tools (e.g. Maze) that track which screen a tester is on. See "Hash routing pattern" below.
4. **Use the design system.** Import styles using relative paths (seven `../` for tool CSS, four for `company.css`; see "Stylesheet link depth"). Use tokens and component classes as the foundation.
5. **Add new styles inline.** If the designer asks for something not in the design system, use `<style>` blocks inside the prototype. Keep the shared design system files untouched.
6. **Hide horizontal scrollbars** on carousels, chip rows, and other horizontal scrollers -- use **`ds-scroll-x`** from `shared.css` (see canvas screen rules). Prefer **`overflow-x: clip`** on the root if the document gets a horizontal scrollbar.
7. **Self-contained.** Everything the prototype needs goes in its folder. No external npm packages or CDN scripts unless the designer explicitly asks.
8. **Look at the design system.** Read the company's existing component HTML files in `public/data/companies/<c>/design-system/components/<c>/` for visual patterns.
9. **One file preferred.** Keep the prototype in a single `index.html` unless it grows large enough to justify splitting.

### Hash routing pattern

All prototypes with multiple views must use hash-based routing so that each view has a distinct URL (e.g. `index.html#dashboard`, `index.html#transfer-form`, `index.html#success`). This enables usability testing tools like Maze to track screen transitions and measure task completion.

**How it works:**

1. Give each view container an `id` prefixed with `view-` (e.g. `id="view-dashboard"`, `id="view-transfer"`, `id="view-success"`).
2. Use a `navigate(viewId)` function instead of directly toggling classes or `hidden`. This function sets `location.hash`, which triggers the `hashchange` listener to show/hide views.
3. Add a `hashchange` listener and an initial route check on `DOMContentLoaded`.

**Standard boilerplate** -- include this in every multi-view prototype:

```html
<script>
  (function () {
    var defaultView = "dashboard";

    function navigate(viewId) {
      location.hash = viewId;
    }

    function applyRoute() {
      var hash = location.hash.slice(1) || defaultView;
      document.querySelectorAll(".view").forEach(function (v) {
        v.classList.toggle("active", v.id === "view-" + hash);
      });
    }

    window.addEventListener("hashchange", applyRoute);
    applyRoute();

    // Expose navigate globally for click handlers
    window.navigate = navigate;
  })();
</script>
```

**CSS for views:**

```css
.view {
  display: none;
}
.view.active {
  display: flex;
} /* or block, grid -- whatever fits the layout */
```

**Using it in click handlers:**

```html
<button onclick="navigate('transfer')">Make a transfer</button>
```

Or in JS:

```js
document.getElementById("btn-next").addEventListener("click", function () {
  navigate("review");
});
```

**Naming conventions for hash values:**

- Use lowercase, hyphenated names: `#dashboard`, `#transfer-form`, `#select-account`, `#review`, `#success`
- Keep them short and descriptive -- they appear in Maze analytics
- For sub-steps within a flow (e.g. picking a "from" account inside a transfer), use a compound name: `#transfer-from`, `#transfer-to`, `#transfer-review`

**Complex prototypes with multiple independent flows:**

Some prototypes have a main view (e.g. sidebar nav switching between Dashboard, Transfers, Payments) plus sub-flows (e.g. a multi-step transfer form). Use a flat hash namespace with descriptive prefixes:

- `#dashboard` -- main dashboard view
- `#transfers` -- transfers hub
- `#transfer-form` -- transfer form step
- `#transfer-from` -- pick "from" account
- `#transfer-to` -- pick "to" account
- `#transfer-review` -- review step
- `#transfer-success` -- confirmation
- `#payments` -- payments hub
- `#pay-bill` -- bill pay form

The `applyRoute()` function can be extended to handle hierarchical state (showing the right sidebar nav item, entering/exiting sub-flows) based on the hash prefix.

**Back button support:** Hash routing gives browser back/forward navigation for free. Users (and Maze testers) can use the back button to return to previous steps.

## Platform-Aware Components

When the designer specifies a target platform (iOS, Android, or desktop), adapt the UI accordingly:

1. **Check the design system first.** Look at `public/data/companies/<c>/design-system/components/<c>/` and `company.css` for existing components that match the platform. If the company has components, always prefer those.
2. **If no matching component exists**, build native-looking UI for the specified platform using the company's brand colors and tokens from `company.css` (or `shared.css` if no company overrides exist):
   - **iOS** -- SF-style navigation bars, large titles, rounded grouped list rows, iOS-style toggles, tab bars with icons, subtle separators, system-like blur effects. Use the company's accent color for tint/interactive elements.
   - **Android / Material** -- Top app bars, FABs, bottom navigation with labels, outlined/filled text fields, Material-style cards with subtle elevation, ripple-like interaction areas. Use the company's primary color for Material theming.
   - **Desktop** -- Sidebar navigation, multi-column layouts, data tables, breadcrumbs, dropdown menus, toolbar actions. Use wider content areas (1024–1280px frames).
3. **Always use the company's brand colors.** Native-looking doesn't mean default blue iOS or purple Material -- it means the platform's layout patterns and interaction idioms rendered in the company's color palette.
4. **If no platform is specified**, default to a generic/neutral style using the design system's existing components.

## Design Tokens (quick reference)

- Backgrounds: `var(--bg-0)` (#ffffff), `var(--bg-1)` (#f8f9fb)
- Accent: `var(--neon-blue)` (#3b82f6), `var(--neon-teal)` (#06b6d4)
- Text: `var(--text)`, `var(--muted)`, `var(--muted-2)` (dark text tokens)
- Border: `var(--border)`
- Radii: `var(--radius-sm)` 10px, `var(--radius-md)` 16px, `var(--radius-lg)` 22px, `var(--radius-xl)` 32px
- Font: Plus Jakarta Sans (loaded via Google Fonts in shared.css)

## Available Component Classes

The design system ships with generic/foundational components. Each company adds its own domain-specific components on top of these.

### Buttons

`.btn`, `.btn-primary`, `.btn-dark`, `.btn-sm`

### Chips

`.ds-chip`, `.ds-chip.is-active`, `.ds-chip-row`

### Tags

`.ds-tag`, `.ds-tag-row`, `.ds-tag-tone-sunset`, `.ds-tag-tone-teal`, `.ds-tag-tone-green`, `.ds-tag-tone-mint`, `.ds-tag-tone-gold`, `.ds-tag-tone-dark`, `.ds-tag-tone-gray`, `.ds-tag-tone-navy`

### Badges & status

`.ds-badge`, `.ds-badge-red`, `.ds-badge-blue`, `.ds-badge-green`, `.ds-status-dot`, `.ds-status-dot-online`, `.ds-status-dot-away`, `.ds-status-dot-offline`

### Form inputs

`.form-group`, `.form-group label`, `.form-group input`, `.ds-search-bar`

### Toggle

`.ds-toggle`, `.ds-toggle.is-on`

### Navigation

